A client that parrots Chrome, as hysteria's does unless told not to.

From hysteria 2.11 the reference client parrots Chrome's QUIC stack by default
(DisableChromeParrot, vendor/hysteria/core/client/client.go:95-103). What that changes above the
wire — the ClientHello, the transport parameters, the connection IDs, how the handshake is read —
is src/quic's. What it changes in how packets are laid out is this patch, behind a new setting,
`chrome_parrot`, which only a client reads:

  - the first Initial packet is numbered 1 (vendor/quic-go/transport.go:265-269); the other two
    spaces start at 0;
  - datagrams start at 1250 bytes, chromeInitialPacketSize (vendor/quic-go/chrome_parrot.go:18-19),
    and the BBR sender is seeded with that — seedPacketSize takes the smaller of what QUIC starts at
    and 1280 (vendor/hysteria/core/internal/congestion/utils.go:45-59);
  - the packet number length follows Chrome's rule: the larger of the unacknowledged range and the
    congestion window in packets, quadrupled, in one, two or four bytes
    (vendor/quic-go/internal/protocol/packet_number.go:59-84), the window counted in the room the
    last datagram's Initial left when that is forty bytes or more
    (vendor/quic-go/internal/ackhandler/sent_packet_handler.go:1014-1039), a record cleared when
    read keys change (vendor/quic-go/connection.go:2054-2067);
  - no two encryption levels share a datagram before the handshake is confirmed
    (noCoalescing, vendor/quic-go/packet_packer.go:185-191, :391-393, :427-429), so an Initial is
    padded alone and never deferred behind the packets that follow it;
  - the ACK of the server's Handshake flight goes in a packet of its own and our Finished in the
    next one, unless what is queued is a retransmission (splitAckFromCrypto,
    vendor/quic-go/packet_packer.go:193-199, :603-610);
  - the CRYPTO of an Initial packet is laid out as the parrot lays it out: a ClientHello too large
    for one packet goes head and tail first (chromeCryptoSplit,
    vendor/quic-go/packet_packer_chaos.go:38-78), and a padded Initial is chaos protected — CRYPTO
    cut at random points, two to ten PINGs, the padding in runs, all shuffled, the packet exactly
    its size (vendor/quic-go/packet_packer_chaos.go:94-274). The SNI scrambling of patch 0005 is
    quic-go's WITHOUT the parrot and is not applied with it (vendor/quic-go/crypto_stream.go:144-163).

The draws come from the connection's PRNG, seeded from the rand callback as quic-go seeds its
packer's PCG from crypto/rand, and are unbiased as Go's IntN is.

Applies on top of 0013.

